			<item>
				<title>Dataclasses in Practice - Fundamentals and Data Modeling in Python</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/posts/dataclasses-in-practice-fundamentals-and-data-modeling-in-python/</link>
				<pubDate>Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/dataclasses-in-practice-fundamentals-and-data-modeling-in-python/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;introduction&#34;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When a Python class exists primarily to represent data, we often write repetitive code. We need to define &lt;code&gt;__init__()&lt;/code&gt;, assign attributes, implement &lt;code&gt;__repr__()&lt;/code&gt; for debugging, and often implement &lt;code&gt;__eq__()&lt;/code&gt; to compare objects.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dataclasses&lt;/strong&gt; reduce this boilerplate and make the intent of the class more explicit.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;what-are-dataclasses&#34;&gt;What are Dataclasses?&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;Dataclass&lt;/strong&gt; is a regular class processed by the &lt;code&gt;@dataclass&lt;/code&gt; decorator. The decorator examines type annotations and adds generated methods such as &lt;code&gt;__init__()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;__repr__()&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;__eq__()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Pydantic in Practice (Part 2) - Intermediate &amp; Advanced Topics with a Real-World Project</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/posts/pydantic-in-practice-part-2-intermediate-advanced-topics-with-a-real-world-project/</link>
				<pubDate>Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/pydantic-in-practice-part-2-intermediate-advanced-topics-with-a-real-world-project/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;recap-of-part-1&#34;&gt;Recap of Part 1&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/pydantic-in-practice-part-1-fundamentals-and-data-validation-in-python/&#34;&gt;Part 1&lt;/a&gt; of this series we covered the fundamentals: what Pydantic is, who created it, installation with &lt;code&gt;uv&lt;/code&gt;, building models with &lt;code&gt;BaseModel&lt;/code&gt;, automatic type validation, the difference between valid/invalid data, and JSON serialization/deserialization. If you haven&amp;rsquo;t read it yet, it&amp;rsquo;s worth starting there - this post assumes you already know how to build a simple model and understand what a &lt;code&gt;ValidationError&lt;/code&gt; is.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here we&amp;rsquo;ll move on to what actually separates &amp;ldquo;basic&amp;rdquo; Pydantic usage from &amp;ldquo;professional&amp;rdquo; production usage: custom validation rules, fine-tuning model behavior, composing complex models, reusable types, and application configuration management. We&amp;rsquo;ll close with a &lt;strong&gt;complete practical project&lt;/strong&gt;, simulating a real order-processing service consuming messages from a queue.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Pydantic in Practice (Part 1) - Fundamentals and Data Validation in Python</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/posts/pydantic-in-practice-part-1-fundamentals-and-data-validation-in-python/</link>
				<pubDate>Wed, 05 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/pydantic-in-practice-part-1-fundamentals-and-data-validation-in-python/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;introduction&#34;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you build APIs, consume external data, or have ever debugged a &lt;code&gt;TypeError&lt;/code&gt; that only showed up in production because a field arrived as a string instead of a number, this post is for you. Let&amp;rsquo;s talk about &lt;strong&gt;Pydantic&lt;/strong&gt;, one of the most widely used libraries in the modern Python ecosystem - one that&amp;rsquo;s probably already running under the hood of some project of yours, even if you never imported it directly (FastAPI, for instance, is built on top of it).&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Sync vs Async in Python - Understanding what&#39;s happening</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/posts/sync-vs-async-in-python-understanding-whats-happening/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/sync-vs-async-in-python-understanding-whats-happening/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;sync-vs-async-in-python---understanding-whats-happening&#34;&gt;Sync vs Async in Python - Understanding What&amp;rsquo;s Happening&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When we start programming in Python, almost everything we write is &lt;code&gt;synchronous&lt;/code&gt;&#xA;(an execution model where operations are performed sequentially, blocking the&#xA;main thread until each task is completed before the next one starts).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And this usually works very well.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You call a function. It executes. Then the next one executes. And so on.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The problem starts when the application needs to talk to the &amp;ldquo;outside world&amp;rdquo;:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
